    {
      "@id": "concept:crimes-amendment-bail-and-sentencing-act-2006-sch-1-sec-15ab",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "15AB Matters to be considered in certain bail applications",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-amendment-bail-and-sentencing-act-2006#sch-1-sec-15ab"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "crimes-amendment-bail-and-sentencing-act-2006.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "crimes-amendment-bail-and-sentencing-act-2006.pdf, p.7",
      "values:originalText": "(1) In determining whether to grant bail to a person charged with, or\nconvicted of, an offence against a law of the Commonwealth, or in\ndetermining conditions to which bail granted to such a person\nshould be subject, a bail authority:\n\n(a) must take into consideration the potential impact of granting\nbail on:\n\n(i) any person against whom the offence is, or was, alleged\nto have been committed; and\n\n(ii) any witness, or potential witness, in proceedings\nrelating to the alleged offence, or offence; and\n\n(b) must not take into consideration any form of customary law\nor cultural practice as a reason for:\n\n(i) excusing, justifying, authorising, requiring or lessening\nthe seriousness of the alleged criminal behaviour to\nwhich the alleged offence relates, or the criminal\nbehaviour to which the offence relates; or\n\n(ii) aggravating the seriousness of the alleged criminal\nbehaviour to which the alleged offence relates, or the\ncriminal behaviour to which the offence relates.\n\nSchedule 1  Amendments\n\n(2) If a person referred to in subparagraph (1)(a)(i) or (ii) is living in,\nor otherwise located in, a remote community, the bail authority\nmust also take into consideration that fact in considering the\npotential impact of granting bail on that person.\n\n(3) In paragraph (1)(b):\ncriminal behaviour includes:\n\n(a) any conduct, omission to act, circumstance or result that is, or\nforms part of, a physical element of the offence in question;\nand\n\n(b) any fault element relating to such a physical element.\n\n(4) To avoid doubt, except as provided by subsections (1) and (2), this\nsection does not affect:\n\n(a) any other matters that a bail authority must, must not or may\ntake into consideration in determining whether to grant bail\nor in determining conditions to which bail should be subject;\nor\n\n(b) the operation of a law of a State or a Territory.\nNote:\nSubsections (1) and (2) indirectly affect laws of the States and\nTerritories because they affect section 68 of the Judiciary Act 1903.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) In determining whether to grant bail to a person charged with, or\nconvicted of, an offence against a law of the Commonwealth, or in\ndetermining conditions to which bail granted to such a person\nshould be subject, a bail authority:\n\n(a) must take into consideration the potential impact of granting\nbail on:\n\n(i) any person against whom the offence is, or was, alleged\nto have been committed; and\n\n(ii) any witness, or potential witness, in proceedings\nrelating to the alleged offence, or offence; and\n\n(b) must not take into consideration any form of customary law\nor cultural practice as a reason for:\n\n(i) excusing, justifying, authorising, requiring or lessening\nthe seriousness of the alleged criminal behaviour to\nwhich the alleged offence relates, or the criminal\nbehaviour to which the offence relates; or\n\n(ii) aggravating the seriousness of the alleged criminal\nbehaviour to which the alleged offence relates, or the\ncriminal behaviour to which the offence relates.\n\nSchedule 1  Amendments\n\n(2) If a person referred to in subparagraph (1)(a)(i) or (ii) is living in,\nor otherwise located in, a remote community, the bail authority\nmust also take into consideration that fact in considering the\npotential impact of granting bail on that person.\n\n(3) In paragraph (1)(b):\ncriminal behaviour includes:\n\n(a) any conduct, omission to act, circumstance or result that is, or\nforms part of, a physical element of the offence in question;\nand\n\n(b) any fault element relating to such a physical element.\n\n(4) To avoid doubt, except as provided by subsections (1) and (2), this\nsection does not affect:\n\n(a) any other matters that a bail authority must, must not or may\ntake into consideration in determining whether to grant bail\nor in determining conditions to which bail should be subject;\nor\n\n(b) the operation of a law of a State or a Territory.\nNote:\nSubsections (1) and (2) indirectly affect laws of the States and\nTerritories because they affect section 68 of the Judiciary Act 1903.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-amendment-bail-and-sentencing-act-2006-sch-1-sec-15ab-ss-1"
        },
